hobbs@uofport.edu (Eric A. Hobbs) writes:

>I see a lot of references to things being unconsititutional at public 
>universities.  What about private universities?  To what extent are these 
>rulings applicable?

Disclaimer: I'm not a lawyer.

Generally the Constitution has legal force only on public universities
in the U.S. The two exceptions:

    There is a law in California saying that private universities that
    take money from the state of California must respect the
    contraints of the First Amendment. (Stanford U.'s speech code was
    recently found to violate this law).

    Some private schools claim they are required to censor
    constitutionally-protected speech in order to obey laws
    such as those against sexual harassment. This is not true.
    No law can require private censorship of constitutionaly-protected
    speech.

Finally, although the Constitution doesn't generally have legal force
on private universities, for many, it has moral force. In other words,
although most private universities could legally violate First
Amendment standards, most choose not to. Indeed many say in writing
(in the Student Code for example) they they will not impose
institutional censorship and that free expression is central to their
mission has a university. When such statements are part of official
university policy they have force of law because they become part
of the contract between student and school.

- Carl

