>: : How liable is the institution for potentially offensive material posted 
>: : or published in those personal pages under current law?  Does the 
>: : disclaimer legally distance the institution at all?
>
>: With respect to universities, universities have a long and respected
>: tradition of providing forums for students. I've don't recall ever
>: hearing of university being required by law to censor students. On the
>: other hand, I have heard of universities being required by law to stop
>: censoring students. I'm enclosing two alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk FAQ's.
>
>I see a lot of references to things being unconsititutional at public 
>universities.  What about private universities?  To what extent are these 
>rulings applicable?
>
The difference is that public universities are considered part of 
state government, and thus subject to both the U.S. and state
constitutions. The bill of rights and other Constitutional guarantees
are essentially limitations on state actions. As such they are applicable
to public institutions, and not to private institutions. One of these
of course is (1st Amdt) freedom of speech (i.e. lack of censorship).

Private institutions on the other hand can violate state and federal
laws. For example, a private school may be found in violation of the 
Civil Rights Act if it were to discriminate on the basis of race, etc.
