>How does receiving financial aid affect this?  If I have a work-study
>job in university X's cafeteria, and I write a novel on a computer
>in a student lab, who can hold the copyright for that work?  My
>employment at the school does not give me access to the tools that
>I used to compose the book on my own time.
>
The legal test in most cases for a "work made for hire" is
essentially that of an employee in the course of his normal
employment. Most financial aid would more than likely not be
considered employment in the legal sense. 

Some of the questions that you can ask about whether or not you
are an employee at any given time are:
1) does the school (or "employer") do normal IRS withholding,
including paying its portion of your social security and medicare?
2) are you covered by workman's comp and/or unemployment for the work?
This latter is more likely state dependant thant the former.

Also, note that the novel must be written in the course of your 
employment. This means that a school might be able to claim a
computer program written by someone hired to program, but in such
a case, usually not the novel.
