In article <3la53f$9un@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu> tinsel@uiuc.edu writes:

>> This came up here a while ago; a student wanted to develop educational
>> multimedia software, have a professor use it for a semester in a course,
>> and then try to sell it for money. We were told that he could not do this
>> without calling in the lawyers and that there would be ownership issues.

>I don't see why.  Professors in all disciplines regularly use
>University resources (office space, computers, etc.) to write
>textbooks, test them on students, and then have them published.
>This is not only allowed, but encouraged/required as part of their
>duties.  However, the University claims no interest in these
>publications.  I fail to see how electronic courseware is any
>different.


First of all, the fact that school's don't traditionally assert rights
to textbooks developed with university resources says nothing as to whether
they *could* assert such rights.  Most likely, they could.

Most research universities do currently have policies regarding various
forms of intellectual developed in the course of one's duties, or using
university resources and asserting an ownership interest by the
institution.  As a UC employee, I can tell you that UC has had such
policies since at least 1967.

There is undoubtedly a pragmatic interest in the marketing of such things
as educational software, that does not exist for the marketing of educational
texts.  Namely, producing a textbook is a high-overhead prospect, and most
texts ain't best-sellers.

Marketing software, on the other hand, is a very low-overhead prospect.
It seems quite natural for the university to be more interested in such.

There's also a difference in that development of software is, typically,
far more facility-intensive than is book-writing; so the institution may
feel a greater moral right to assert an ownership interest in software,
than in books.


Whether you like it or not, I wouldn't bet on a court finding that the
institution has no such interest.  Consequently, I'd recommend that anybody
undertaking such a project be *sure* to find out what the university's
position is *before* starting...



