Many universities, particularly state universities, have something in fine
print somewhere restricting use of their facilities to education, research,
and other non-profit activities. 

If you produced a novel on University equipment, and sold it for a big
profit, becoming rich and famous, and admitted writing it on University
equipment, there might be grounds for the University lawyers to try to get
a chunk of it.

This came up here a while ago; a student wanted to develop educational
multimedia software, have a professor use it for a semester in a course,
and then try to sell it for money. We were told that he could not do this
without calling in the lawyers and that there would be ownership issues.

NOTE: this project was not done, so I don't have any official citations.
This was just the opinion of the people involved, and my interpretation of
what I was told. I'm just suggesting that if you use university equipment
for personal projects, you are getting into a grey area.

