I would ask though whether they were actually employees or independant
contractors. This was the distinction made by the Supreme Court in
CCNV v Reed in determining whether a work was a "work for hire".

Different courts have used different tests. However, the general thrust
is to determine whether or not the person is treated like an employee.
Thus, if you aren't paying taxes on someone, he can often make a good
argument that he is not an employee, but instead an independant contractor.
