From caf-talk Caf Mar 31 20:52:32 1995
From: nrh.primenet.com@PrimeNet.Com (Mary-Mark Haggard)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Why Homeschool Your High Schooler Pt1
Date: 31 Mar 1995 20:52:28 -0500
Message-ID: <nrh.primenet.com-3103951748550001@ip009.lax.primenet.com.796701146>

I am a mother of six, three are teenagers.  I¹m not worried about gangs,
drug or alcohol abuse or whether my daughter might get pregnant.  It
hasn¹t crossed my mind that they could get a venereal disease.  Fashion
has never been an issue.  Not that any of these things wouldn¹t loom in
the future, it¹s just that I haven¹t had to worry about them.  Why? 
Because my children don¹t go to school.
The typical high school day begins about 8 AM and goes to 3 PM.  There are
generally one to three hours of homework a night.  Nine of the most
productive hours of the day are spent doing school work and transportation
time is added.  After three to four years of this kind of time devotion, a
student graduates and is suppose to be ready to go to work or college.
Through these years the high schooler has had little time for family and
chores, especially if you add extracurricular activities and socializing. 
A teacher once told my mother, who had twelve children, that since her
oldest daughter was now in high school, she should not be trying to rely
on her for household help.
While the subjects taught in school -  Math, English, Language, History
and Science, are valuable, many students will tell you they rarely pay
attention in class.  Many teachers will admit they have a great deal of
trouble commanding attention.  Most of the activity is crowd control, not
learning.  Thus the need for extensive homework.  Most students learn best
when they can sit and concentrate by themselves.  As John Holt, a public
school teacher for twenty years, said, ³Children do not learn well in the
excitement of the peer environment.²
The parents in one school district in Northern California tried to get a
rule passed of no homework.  It failed.  But it brings to surface the
families¹ frustrations, that the children don¹t have enough time for other
things they think are valuable.  It failed because homework is a good
percentage of where the real learning takes place.  A dilemma indeed.  A
better idea would be to do the homework, one to three hours a day, at
home, in the morning when the student is fresh and eliminate school.  This
is what home schoolers do.
Some may find it hard to believe, but there is plenty of evidence that
home schoolers are succeeding nicely academically without all the hours a
schooled child has to put in.  Laurel Springs, a private school for home
schoolers, says that many colleges are requesting applications from home
schoolers.  They not only find them well educated but say home schoolers
show more individuality and initiative than the average schooled teenager.
Home schoolers are testing well on the SAT and Dr. Brian Ray, president of
the National Home Education Research Institute, conducted nationwide study
in 1990 of 1,500 families with 4,600 children.  He found these home
educated children to average at or above the 80th percentile in all
subject areas.  The national average of all conventionally schooled
children is the 50th percentile.
Clearly a child can receive a good academic education at home and in a
third less time than their counterparts in school.
This is the next point, time.  Time is of the essence, why waste it.  Some
of that extra time can be used to help around the house.  It teaches the
child responsibility, discipline and a sense of belonging and being
needed.  The children are not raised with the attitude that the world owes
them a living, which is what is being taught in many schools today.  They
have to pull their weight and their contribution is important.  This
produces strong families, which in turn are good for society as a whole.
There is an even more dynamic aspect of the value of the saved time.  What
happens and what can happen to that time.  First it can relieve some of
the stress many high school students feel today.  Most importantly, it can
be used for what they want.  To do what interests them.  To dream and
fulfill those dreams.  They can develop their own interests and God given
talents and abilities.  Their time is not pilfered away doing busy work in
study areas that will never be something they want to follow up on.  They
can reach the heights of investigation and pursuit.  This is one of the
greatest advantages of home schooling.  One teenager from Whitier
California wrote an opera.  Several won fiddle and banjo competitions,
competing against adults.  One child has read extensively on herbs and
natural healing and binds the wounds and treats many of the families¹
ailments with herbs grown in her garden.  One fifteen year old is the
youngest person in the country to run the electronics at a local radio
station.  Some have started their own businesses.  One teenager videotapes
the basketball and soccer games at a local park, edits the tapes to music
and then sells them to the parents of the children. 
The girls especially have the time and opportunity to learn and practice
the feminine arts of cooking, sewing, child care and running a home.  You
can see them blossoming in their roles as rulers of the world.
These teenagers are trying and succeeding to find their way in the world,
so that they can be creative and productive members of society.  This,
after all, should be one of the most important goals of education.  Home
schooling provides it naturally and beautifully.
During the teen years, between childhood and adulthood, the individual has
time to explore many possibilities.  They are not totally responsible for
their own livelihood, but they are certainly quite capable to learn and do
many things on an adult level.  Apprenticing has regained some popularity
of late.  A home schooler has the time to work, perhaps a few hours a
week, in an architect¹s office, doing plastering or carpentry on the job,
or even helping in a dentist¹s office.  It¹s a shame to wait until you are
twenty to twenty-five years old after schooling to find out you don¹t
really like dentistry, or working in an office, etc.  This, of course,
does hot mean that they are tied to these possibilities.  It does give
them a jump start to look around and try career ideas.
There is one very important piece to this puzzle of time and education. 
Television watching must be largely eliminated.  Getting rid of the TV is
the best option.  If not, be aware that the more time away from the TV is
more time possible for positive growth.  If your children do watch
television regularly, schooled of home schooled, they will be limiting
their creative and dynamic initiatives.
While homeschooling teenagers has its challenges, the time saved and used
in more positive ways is a joy to the family and can give a sense of
accomplishment to the student.

